let __v_153 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Two truths and a lie: each of you says three things about yourself, two true and one made up, and the rest guess the lie - a point for each correct guess, and you tell them the answer before the next person goes. Three is the right number of players upwards, and the lies get better by the second round.\",\"Would you rather: one of you puts two bad options against each other and everybody has to pick one and say why in a sentence - 'would you rather be ten minutes late every day or an hour late once a week'. The reasons are the fun part, not the choice.\",\"Twenty questions: one person thinks of a person, a place or a thing, the others ask questions that can only be answered yes or no, and twenty questions has to be enough. It works in a car, at a table, and with children from about six.\",\"The alphabet game for a journey: find each letter of the alphabet in order on road signs, number plates or shop fronts, and nobody may use a word twice. First to Z wins, and the hard letters are the fun ones.\"]}")?;
frame.store("GROUP_GAMES", __v_153.clone())?;
let __v_154 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Draw an animal wearing a hat it would never choose. Give them three minutes and no sharpener: the limit is what gets a drawing finished, and a finished silly one beats a perfect blank page.\",\"Draw the kitchen as a mouse would see it - everything enormous and books on the floor. One shape to start with stops the 'I don't know what to draw' that a blank page causes.\",\"Draw a house for the person sitting next to you: what is on the roof, what is behind the door. Ask them what is missing afterwards and let them add it in pen.\",\"Draw the same mug three times: once in one line without lifting the pen, once looking only at the mug and never at the paper, once in under a minute. Three bad drawings beat one careful blank page, and the second one usually surprises you.\"]}")?;
frame.store("DRAWING_PROMPTS", __v_154.clone())?;
let __v_155 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[\"Someone finds a key that fits no lock they own, and keeps it for a month before they find out what it opens. Two hundred words, no planning, and do not give it an ending - stop where the interesting part starts.\",\"Write the same small scene twice: once from the person who is late, once from the person waiting. Nothing has to happen in either; the two ways of seeing the same ten minutes are the whole exercise.\",\"A character has to tell a lie to somebody they love, on a day that is otherwise ordinary. Write the ten minutes before they do it and leave the lie out of the page.\",\"Describe a room from the point of view of the smell in it. Three hundred words, and no person may be named: it forces the details out of you.\"]}")?;
